.TH TASKUI 1 "August 2026" "taskui 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
taskui \- folding, searchable front end for go\-task
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B taskui
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B taskui
browses the Taskfile in
.IR DIR
(the current directory by default), runs tasks from it, and keeps their output
around to fold and search afterwards \(em both while a task is running and
across previous runs.
.PP
It requires
.BR task (1)
from https://taskfile.dev on
.BR PATH ;
everything it knows comes from asking go\-task rather than parsing Taskfiles
itself.
.PP
Existing Taskfile front ends are launchers: they pick a task, exit, and hand the
terminal over.
.B taskui
is meant to be the thing you sit in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-list
Print the discovered tasks and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump " MODE"
Print a pivot fully expanded and exit.
.I MODE
is
.B domain
or
.BR verb .
.TP
.BI \-\-graph " TASK"
Print the execution graph reachable from
.I TASK
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " TASK"
Run
.I TASK
without a terminal and print the captured tree. The run is stored as if it had
been run interactively.
.TP
.BI \-\-args " ARGS"
Arguments for
.BR \-\-run ,
split shell\-style, so
.RB \(dq \-\- \(dq
and quoted values survive.
.TP
.B \-\-last
Open the most recent stored run for this project instead of the picker.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " PATTERN"
Search every stored run for
.I PATTERN
and exit. Works from any directory, since it reads the archive rather than the
project.
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot " WxH"
Render one frame to standard output and exit. With
.B \-\-run
the keys given to
.B \-\-keys
are played into the live run, so interactive flows can be driven from a script.
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " KEYS"
Keys to press before a
.BR \-\-screenshot .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read colours and keybindings from
.I PATH
instead of the default location.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-config
Print every colour and keybinding at its default, annotated, and exit. Redirect
it to create a config file to edit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.B ?
inside taskui lists every binding, grouped by context, and is authoritative:
it and the footer are generated from one table, so neither can drift. The
bindings below are the defaults.
.SS Picker
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " gg ", " G ", " ^d ", " ^u
Move; first and last row; half a screen.
.TP
.BR space ", " o
Fold or unfold the group under the cursor.
.TP
.BR O ", " Tab
Fold or unfold everything.
.TP
.B Enter
Run the task.
.TP
.B a
Run it with arguments. Variables the task declares with
.B requires
are pre\-filled.
.TP
.B s
Show what the task is and what it will run, before running it.
.TP
.B p
Toggle the pivot: group by domain or by verb.
.TP
.B /
Filter the list down to matching tasks.
.TP
.B t
Jump to a task, leaving the list intact.
.TP
.B i
Arm interactive mode for the next run.
.TP
.B F
Arm
.BR \-\-force ,
so go\-task's up\-to\-date checks do not skip the run.
.TP
.B v
Go to whatever is running. A live slot you are already on keeps the screen; failing
that, the earliest\-started live slot; and with nothing running at all, the last
run.
.TP
.B h
Past runs.
.TP
.B x
Stop the run belonging to the task under the cursor, wherever its slot is.
Press it again to escalate to
.BR SIGKILL .
From here nothing is on screen, so this is how a background run is stopped
without switching to it first.
.TP
.B K
Stop every run, staying in taskui. Asks first.
.SS Run
.TP
.BR space ", " o
How much of a task's output is on screen, in three states: hidden, a peek at the
last few lines, then all of it. A peek is the resting state \(em see
.B "Peek windows"
under NOTES.
.TP
.B O
Move every task in the run through the same three states at once.
.TP
.B /
Search the output;
.B n
and
.B N
step through matches.
.TP
.B f
Filter to matching lines only;
.B [
and
.B ]
change how much context each match keeps.
.TP
.B r
Re\-run this task with the same arguments, and the same flags the run was started
with.
.TP
.B R
The same, with
.BR \-\-force :
a task go\-task considers up to date otherwise declines to run. The override only
adds, so plain
.B r
on a run that was already forced stays forced.
.TP
.B i
Type at the running task. This works even when the prompt is not visible.
.TP
.B I
Re\-run interactively, so prompts are visible from the start.
.TP
.B W
Watch: re\-run whenever the source changes.
.TP
.B x
Stop the run, signalling the whole process group with
.BR SIGTERM .
Press it again while it is still going to escalate to
.BR SIGKILL ,
which cannot be caught.
.TP
.B K
Stop every open run, not just this one. Asks first.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab ", " 1 \- 9
Switch between open runs. Runs live in slots, one per task name, and each
slot keeps its own scroll position and folds.
.TP
.B X
Close the slot. Only once its run has stopped.
.TP
.BR y ", " Y
Copy the line under the cursor, or everything the task printed.
.TP
.B Esc
Back to the picker. Every run keeps going.
.SS History
.TP
.B Enter
Reopen the run.
.TP
.B /
Search across every stored run.
.TP
.B a
Widen to all projects.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/taskui/config.yaml
Colours, keybindings and
.BR peek\-lines .
Defaults to
.IR ~/.config/taskui/config.yaml .
The file is optional, and so is every key in it.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/taskui/runs
Stored runs, most recent 50. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/state/taskui/runs .
One directory per run, holding a
.I manifest.json
and, per task, the ANSI\-stripped output and a sidecar keeping the escape
sequences. The stripped half is plain text on purpose: the archive is meant to
be greppable without taskui. Created with owner\-only permissions.
.TP
.I .taskui-danger
In the project root. One pattern per line, where
.B *
matches any characters and
.B #
starts a comment. Matching tasks need a confirmation before they run. The
presence of this file replaces taskui's own guess entirely.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
.TQ
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Override where the config and the run archive live.
.PP
Runs are started with
.BR CARGO_TERM_COLOR ,
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
and
.B FORCE_COLOR
set, because output is captured through a pipe and tools that auto\-detect a
terminal would otherwise drop their colour.
.SH NOTES
.SS Peek windows
A task at rest shows a window on the last few lines it printed, marked
.BR ▿ .
Folded away entirely is
.BR ▸ ,
fully open is
.BR ▾ ,
and
.B o
walks the three in that order.
.PP
The window is on the
.I end
of the output, and it tails: a command puts its verdict
last, a task still running has its news at the bottom, and a peeking
.B "docker compose logs \-f"
is a live five\-line view of the stack while you work elsewhere. Lines in a peek
are cut at the edge rather than wrapped, because five lines has to mean five
lines; opening the task fully brings wrapping back.
.PP
Following moves the cursor and nothing else. It does not open whatever is running:
every task already peeks, so the live one is showing its latest output where it
sits, and expanding it would push every other window off the screen. A fold you set
yourself is left alone. The cursor also tracks the line it is on rather than a row
number, so output arriving in an earlier task does not slide the view.
.PP
.B peek\-lines
in
.I config.yaml
sets the size. The default is 5.
.SS Credentials
.BR "task \-\-summary" ,
which taskui runs to build the execution graph, prints the resolved
environment. For a Taskfile using
.B dotenv
that is live credentials. Values that look like secrets are masked before a line
reaches the screen, the buffers or the disk, and the run view reports how many
were masked. Zero means none were found, not that the output was checked and
cleared.
.SS Long\-running tasks
Tasks that never end \(em
.BR "docker compose up" ,
a dev server, a
.B tail \-f
\(em are supported. Runs live in slots, one per task name, so starting a
different task parks the current one rather than stopping it, and every parked
run keeps draining and archives itself when it ends. Output is capped at 20,000
lines per task, dropped from the oldest end and counted on the task's row.
.PP
Because most slots are not on screen, the picker says what is live: a task with a
running slot shows
.B ▶
and a ticking elapsed time where its last outcome would go, and
.B v
goes to whichever slot is running rather than to the one you left last.
.PP
Two limits. A run that never ends is never archived until you stop it with
.BR x ,
and there is no detach:
.B q
stops every slot, so a container is never left behind by a taskui that has
exited. Because that reaches runs you are not looking at,
.B q
asks first whenever anything is still going.
.SS Stopping
.B x
signals the run's whole process group with
.BR SIGTERM :
killing the
.B task
process alone leaves the shell commands beneath it running, and they are what is
doing the work.
.PP
.B SIGTERM
can be caught, and things catch it \(em a
.B trap
in a shell script, a runtime waiting on a call that will not return. A second
.B x
escalates to
.BR SIGKILL ,
which cannot be. Anything still in the group a moment after go\-task itself has
gone is taken with
.B SIGKILL
regardless, so a command that ignores
.B SIGTERM
does not outlive the run that started it. That only applies to a run you
stopped: one that ends on its own may have left something behind deliberately,
which is the entire point of
.BR "docker compose up \-d" .
.PP
The one case nothing covers is taskui being killed from outside.
.B "kill \-9"
runs no destructors, in any program, so an externally killed taskui does leave
its build running.
.PP
To run tasks against a stack you have up, keep the finite part separate from the
infinite one \(em
.B "docker compose up \-d \-\-wait"
exits once healthy and can be a
.BR deps: ,
while
.B "docker compose logs \-f"
owns a slot. Use
.B "exec \-T"
in the tasks that reach into the stack: every command runs behind go\-task's
prefixing pipe, so
.B exec
without
.B \-T
refuses for want of a TTY.
.SS Interactive tasks
Under go\-task's prefixing writer an unterminated prompt is never flushed, so a
task waiting for input appears to hang. Standard input still reaches it, so
.B i
answers it blind;
.B I
re\-runs the task with interleaved output so prompts are visible, at the cost of
attributing lines by the last task to announce itself rather than per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the task that was run, or 0 when taskui exits without
running anything.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Browse another project:
.PP
.RS
.EX
taskui ~/src/myrepo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find when something started failing, without opening the interface:
.PP
.RS
.EX
taskui \-\-search 'migration.*pending'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a task with arguments and print the captured tree:
.PP
.RS
.EX
taskui \-\-run backend:test \-\-args '\-\- \-p ingest'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Answer a prompt from a script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
taskui \-\-run deploy \-\-screenshot 100x20 \-\-keys $'iy\en'
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR task (1),
https://taskfile.dev
.PP
